## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a stable online statistic with clear time/space guarantees using simple data structures.

## Key Concepts

- streaming algorithms
- two-heap median maintenance
- amortized vs worst-case costs
- space efficiency

## Code Example

```javascript
// production-ready sketch of two-heap median tracker
class BinaryHeap {
  constructor(compare){ this.data=[]; this.compare=compare; }
  size(){ return this.data.length; }
  top(){ return this.data[0]; }
  push(x){ this.data.push(x); this._siftUp(this.data.length-1); }
  pop(){ const top=this.data[0]; const end=this.data.pop(); if (this.data.length){ this.data[0]=end; this._siftDown(0); } return top; }
  _siftUp(i){ const x=this.data[i]; while(i>0){ const p=(i-1)>>1; if (this.compare(x, this.data[p])>=0) break; this.data[i]=this.data[p]; i=p; } this.data[i]=x; }
  _siftDown(i){ const n=this.data.length; const x=this.data[i]; while(true){ let l=2*i+1, r=l+1, smallest=i; if (l<n && this.compare(this.data[l], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=l; if (r<n && this.compare(this.data[r], this.data[smallest])<0) smallest=r; if (smallest===i) break; this.data[i]=this.data[smallest]; i=smallest; } this.data[i]=x; }
}

class MedianTracker {
  constructor(){ this.lower=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> b-a); this.upper=new BinaryHeap((a,b)=> a-b); }
  insert(x){ if (this.lower.size()===0 || x <= this.lower.top()) this.lower.push(x); else this.upper.push(x);
    if (this.lower.size() > this.upper.size()+1) this.upper.push(this.lower.pop());
    else if (this.upper.size() > this.lower.size()) this.lower.push(this.upper.pop());
  }
  median(){ if (this.lower.size() === this.upper.size()) return (this.lower.top() + this.upper.top())/2; return this.lower.top(); }
}
```

